<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 10 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">Civilisations of the Andes</h1><p class="path-tagline">From Caral's platform mounds to the last Inca stronghold at Vilcabamba, the civilisations of the Andes.</p><p class="path-intro">Caral–Supe, on Peru's north-central coast, raised platform mounds from about 3000 BCE and is the oldest known civilisation of the Americas. Later Andean peoples built on what came before: Chavín's temple cult spread across the central Andes, Paracas is believed to have evolved into the Nazca culture, and Wari's roads and cord records foreshadowed the institutions of the Incas. The stops move between the desert coast, where Moche lords and the kings of Chimor ruled irrigated valleys, and the highlands of Wari and Tiwanaku. They end with Tawantinsuyu, which stretched some 4,000 km along the Andes, and the Neo-Inca State that held out at Vilcabamba until 1572.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Builder</a> · <a href="/start">the Timekeeper</a></p><p class="path-print-row"><button type="button" class="ws-print path-print">Print this path</button></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/culture/caral-supe-civilisation"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/caral.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Profile · c. 3000 BCE – c. 1800 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Caral–Supe civilisation</span><span class="path-step-why">As many as thirty centres in three small coastal valleys raised stepped platform mounds and sunken plazas from about 3000 BCE, with no pottery and no evidence of warfare.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/chavin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lanzon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Profile · 950 BCE – 400 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Chavín</span><span class="path-step-why">A highland temple town of c. 950–400 BCE whose priests drew pilgrims from coast, mountains and forest, and whose art of jaguars, eagles and caimans spread far across Peru.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/culture/paracas-culture"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trepanning.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Profile · c. 800 BCE – c. 100 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Paracas culture</span><span class="path-step-why">On Peru's south coast its dead were wrapped in layers of embroidered cloth, the Paracas Necropolis textiles, among the finest of the Pre-Columbian Andes.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/nazca-lines"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nazca-lines.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Encyclopedia · Work · c. 500 BCE–500 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Nazca Lines</span><span class="path-step-why">Figures and lines scraped into the desert between the Nazca and Palpa valleys, mostly between about 500 BCE and 500 CE, by the Paracas and Nazca cultures.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Nazca Lines are huge geoglyphs scraped into the desert surface between the Nazca and Palpa valleys of southern Peru, made mostly between about 500 BCE and 500 CE by the Paracas and Nazca cultures.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/moche"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lord-of-sipan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Profile · 100 CE – 800 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Moche</span><span class="path-step-why">On Peru's north coast, c. 100–800 CE, lords ruled irrigated valleys from adobe platforms such as the Huaca del Sol, and potters made lifelike portrait vessels.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/wari-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pikillacta.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Profile · 600 CE – 1000 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Wari Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Often called the first Andean empire, Wari planted planned centres across highland Peru from about 600, and its roads and cord records foreshadowed the Incas.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/tiwanaku"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kalasasaya.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Profile · 500 CE – 1000 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Tiwanaku</span><span class="path-step-why">A ceremonial city at about 3,850 metres near Lake Titicaca, sustained by raised fields and llama caravans, whose Staff God imagery spread across the southern Andes.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/chan-chan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chan-chan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Encyclopedia · Place · c. 850–1470</span><span class="path-step-name">Chan Chan</span><span class="path-step-why">The Chimú capital near Trujillo, the largest adobe city of the pre-Columbian Americas, with some ten walled royal compounds; the Incas took it around 1470.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Chan Chan was the capital of the Chimú kingdom on the north coast of Peru, near modern Trujillo, and the largest adobe city of the pre-Columbian Americas.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/inca-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/machu-picchu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Profile · 1438 CE – 1533 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Inca Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Tawantinsuyu stretched some 4,000 km and ran on labour tribute, storehouses, a 40,000 km road network and khipu records, until Pizarro's conquest in 1532–1533.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Inca Empire was the largest state in pre-Columbian America, centred on Cusco in the Andes of South America.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/neo-inca-state"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/espiritu-pampa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">10 · Profile · 1537 CE – 1572 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Neo-Inca State</span><span class="path-step-why">Founded by Manco Inca in 1537, it kept the Inca court and Sun cult alive at Vilcabamba until Spanish forces took it and executed Túpac Amaru in 1572.</span></span></a></li></ol>
